Important before you publish this
There are a few things I would not blindly copy from the old Staff App policy into the Family App policy.

1. Remove staff-specific language
Your old policy talks about:

Nurse registration
Staff credentials
Nurse shifts
OTP attendance
Facility geofencing
Nurse check-in locations
Caretaker designation
Those are primarily Staff App functions and shouldn't be presented as Family App data collection unless the Family App actually collects them.

2. Verify your actual SDKs
Before publishing, check your pubspec.yaml/pubspec.lock and Android/iOS configuration for things such as:

Firebase
Razorpay
AI provider
Crash reporting
Analytics
Voice/transcription services
File storage
Any location SDK
Any advertising SDK
Google Play requires your Privacy Policy and Data Safety declaration to accurately represent the data practices of the app and its SDKs. (Google Help)

3. Be especially careful with AI
Your Family App has the AI Care Coordinator, so the final policy needs to identify what happens to messages, voice recordings, uploaded files, and health information sent to the AI provider. Google specifically states that its User Data requirements also apply to third-party AI integrations. (Google Help)

4. Don't claim encryption/security features unless they're actually implemented
For example, if your backend/database isn't encrypted at rest, don't state:

"All healthcare data is encrypted at rest."

Instead, describe the protections you actually use.

5. Google Play requirements
Because ProCare handles health information, you should also complete the Health Apps declaration in Play Console. Google says this declaration applies to apps distributed through Play, including testing tracks, and health-related apps must accurately disclose their health functionality. (Google Help)

Also make sure your Privacy Policy, Play Store Data Safety form, permissions, and actual code/SDK behavior all match. Google explicitly requires these disclosures to be comprehensive and accurate. (Google Help)

If you give me your ProCare Family pubspec.yaml, I can also map the actual Flutter packages/SDKs to the Google Play Data Safety form (data type → collected/shared → purpose → required/optional → retention/deletion), which is the next thing I'd recommend doing before submitting the app.

